### 4.2 Build roots and the closed `go-v1` command

`build_roots` and build commands exist only in manifest schema 6. Every build
root MUST be a portable relative path other than `.`, MUST name a real,
link-free directory in the immutable raw snapshot, and MUST be unique and
pairwise disjoint. No build root may equal, contain, or be contained by a
runtime root. Every declared build root MUST be referenced by at least one
build command.

A build command has exactly this package-controlled surface:

```json
{"type":"build","driver":"go-v1","source_dir":"build/cmd/tool"}
```

The object MUST contain exactly `type`, `driver`, and `source_dir`; the driver
MUST be the closed identifier `go-v1`. `source_dir` MUST be a real, link-free
directory below exactly one declared build root and MAY equal that root, but
MUST NOT be `.`. The containing build root is the command's `build_root`; it
MUST contain `go.mod` directly, and that file MUST be the nearest ancestor
`go.mod` of `source_dir`. An intervening module is invalid.

The manager MUST derive the artifact-relative path solely from the command
name: `bin/<command>` on Unix and `bin/<command>.exe` on Windows. A package
MUST NOT select a program, arguments, environment, tags, flags, toolchain,
output path, build script, hook, plugin, generator, or post-build action.
Unsupported drivers MUST fail without falling back to a system command or
generic build facility. Build commands participate in existing activation,
dependency-command selection, portable-name collision, shim collision, and
verify before shared installation state changes. Publication writes a complete
immutable cache entry and applies project, runtime, shim, adapter, and consumer
changes in one serialized journaled transaction. A failed preflight or build
discards private staging and leaves the existing installation, consumers, and
live cache unchanged.

### Fixed `go-v1` prerequisites

A schema 6 Go module must satisfy all of these constraints:

- `build_roots` names dedicated, real, link-free directories below the source
  snapshot. A root cannot be `.`, overlap a runtime root, or be unused.
- Each build root contains `go.mod` directly, and it is the nearest ancestor
  module for each command's `source_dir`. The source directory is a real,
  link-free directory below exactly one build root.
- The selected package is exactly one native package named `main`.
- Dependencies are complete under the module's checked-in `vendor/` tree.
  Network resolution, `go mod` mutation or download, workspaces, toolchain
  switching, and cross-compilation are unsupported.
- Curator selects an operator-trusted `<GOROOT>/bin/go` independently of the
  package. It accepts Go 1.23 or newer only when that release family has been
  tested by the manager against the `go-v1` vectors. Ordering a newer unknown
  family after a supported release is not enough.
[`agent-skill-v6` case](../../conformance/v1/schema-cases/agent-skill-v6/valid.json)
and legacy
[`csk-skill-v6` case](../../conformance/v1/schema-cases/csk-skill-v6/valid.json).

For every build command:

- `build_roots` must contain a dedicated, real, link-free module directory
  below the immutable snapshot. It cannot contain `.`, overlap a runtime root,
  or remain unused.
- `source_dir` must be a real, link-free directory below exactly one build
  root. That build root contains its nearest ancestor `go.mod` directly.
- The module must be self-contained for `-mod=vendor`; all non-standard
  dependencies and embedded inputs remain below the build root. Network module
  resolution, workspaces, and toolchain switching are unavailable.
- The only package-controlled build fields are exactly `type`, `driver`, and
  `source_dir`. The protocol defines only `driver: "go-v1"`. There is no
  package argument, environment, flag, tag, toolchain, output, hook, plugin,
  generator, test, or build-script field.
- `go-v1` requires one native `main` package, an operator-trusted Go 1.23 or
  newer release family that the manager has tested against the driver vectors,
  vendored dependencies, native `gc`, internal linking, and no cgo, PGO,
9. only after all preceding gates pass, resolve and fingerprint a trusted
   toolchain, derive build inputs and logical cache keys, validate the protected
   cache boundary, and inspect eligible receipts and artifacts read-only.

Snapshot validation MUST reject links, special files, invalid protocol paths,
duplicate encoded paths, and platform path collisions. A build root MUST be a
real link-free directory below the snapshot, MUST NOT be `.`, MUST be disjoint
from every runtime root, and MUST contain the nearest ancestor `go.mod` of each
of its commands' `source_dir` values. Every declared build root MUST be used.
Generated artifacts remain only in manager-owned staging and immutable cache
state and MUST NOT become agent-facing context.

Audit and registry gates therefore precede both persistent cache lookup and
compilation. Neither a cache hit nor a dry run may bypass source validation,
static context exclusion, build-source hashing, closure construction, collision
checks, audit, attestation, revocation, or moved-tag evaluation.

